On a line that holds content or ends in a forced break, Ladybird counts
the strut of every inline box that’s on the line: its font and line
height, aligned by its vertical-align. That includes a box with no text
on the line: an empty box, a box whose whitespace collapsed, and a box
around the line’s forced break or around a block-level box next to the
line. A line that holds nothing but such boxes keeps zero height. An
empty box is on the line that holds the content before it, also when
that line wraps right after it.

Blink does the same. LogicalLineBuilder::HandleOpenTag() computes the
text metrics of every inline box it opens. For the boxes still open at
the start of a line, InlineLayoutStateStack::OnBeginPlaceItems()
restores those metrics. And on a line with nothing else on it,
SetIsEmptyLine() makes AddBoxFragmentPlaceholder() give a box no block
size. In WebKit, InlineLevelBox::mayStretchLineBox() lets an inline box
stretch the line box whether or not it has content. In Gecko, each span
gets its minimum line height in nsLineLayout::VerticalAlignFrames(),
unless that sets its zeroEffectiveSpanBox flag for the span. And
VerticalAlignLine() gives an empty line (mLineIsEmpty) no height,
through SetSpanForEmptyLine(). All of that is from reading the sources.

In quirks mode, Ladybird gives an inline box without text no strut,
unless it has a border or padding at its start or end, or it’s around a
forced break. That’s the line-height-calculation quirk as Blink and
WebKit apply it. HandleOpenTag() skips the metrics of an item that
IsEmptyItem(), and LogicalLineBuilder::PlaceControlItem() calls
EnsureTextMetrics() for the box around a forced break. WebKit decides
the same two things in InlineQuirks::inlineBoxAffectsLineBox() and
lineBreakBoxAffectsParentInlineBox(). Gecko’s zeroEffectiveSpanBox also
counts a box with a margin, and leaves out a box around a forced break
that follows text on its line. Blink applies the quirk in limited-quirks
mode too (Document::InLineHeightQuirksMode()). Ladybird doesn’t: Its
line builder knows quirks mode only.

Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and Playwright’s WebKit 26.5 ran
the cases of the three tests added here. Two of those tests are
inline-box-struts.html and its quirks-mode twin. In standards mode, the
three agree on all 85 cases, and Ladybird gives what they give: the
heights with an empty box under each vertical-align value, with nested
boxes, in vertical writing modes, beside blocks, at soft wraps and
forced breaks, and the position of the empty box on its line. In quirks
mode, Ladybird gives what Chrome and WebKit give on all 85 cases of that
test, and Firefox differs from those two engines on some of them.

A third test, inline-box-strut-client-rects.html, has 12 cases of where
the client rects of such a box are: after the start edges of the boxes
around it, on a justified line, and on each of two lines that end in
forced breaks. Ladybird gives what WebKit gives on all 12 cases. Chrome
differs on one, where it adds a rect for a forced break. And Firefox
differs on two, where it widens a justified space by 2px less.

The same run found differences that the tests leave out, and that this
change doesn’t address. A tall inline box with vertical-align:middle or
vertical-align:text-top makes a 60px line 67px or 89px tall, with text
in the box or without. A wbr element inside an inline box gives no soft
wrap opportunity. An empty box is one space off in the inline direction
where it follows a hanging space under white-space:pre-wrap, and where
it sits between left-to-right text in a right-to-left block. And in
quirks mode, a line holding only an inline-block or only a forced break
keeps the strut of its block container.
